5.2. Operational block
The structural diagram of the operational block (OB) is shown in Fig.8.
The OB is intended to perform the following functions:
  Address calculation and its temporary storage in the address register;
  Receiving data and storing them in registers;
  Performing arithmetic and logical operations between registers and between registers and constants;
  Outputting data to the system bus;
  Forming interrupt vector addresses;
  Forming states,

The OB includes:
  Aata buffer register (BRD);
  Address register, instruction counter copy register and address comparator (RA, KRSK, KOMP);
  OB instruction buffer register (BRK OB);
  OB instruction register (RK OB);
  Constants block;
  Processor status register, processor status copy register (RSP, KRSP);
  General-purpose registers, accumulator, and source register (RONY, AK, RI);
  16-bit arithmetic-logic unit (ALU);
  Shifter (SDV);
  Byte exchange block and write circuit (BOB and Skhed).

Data are received into the BRD from external 37.
When writing to external memory, in the BRD the data are prepared for transfer.

In the address register (RA), the formed address is written for transmission to the system bus during exchanges.
Since in the PRC commands are fetched in advance, then by the end
of execution of the current command the next command has already been received into the BRK,
i.e., this command can no longer be programmatically modified.
